{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775378513-FRUm9W49gP0QXPtYZBKz","role":"assistant","created":1775378520,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see that the Treasury notes of 1890 were being retired. The document mentions:\n- \"The Treasury notes of 1890 have been in process of retirement since March 1900\"\n- On October 20, 1961, $1 million of Treasury notes of 1890 were determined to be destroyed or irretrievably lost\n\nBut I need to find when they were \"removed from the U.S. federal government ledgers\" - which seems to be the year when they were fully retired or removed from the books.\n\nLet me look at more of the data to understand what happened. Looking at the data:\n- In 1961: Treasury notes of 1890 = 1 (million)\n- In 1962: Treasury notes of 1890 = * (which means less than $500,000)\n\nSo it looks like the Treasury notes of 1890 were removed from the ledgers in 1962 (they went from $1 million to essentially zero). The asterisk in the 1962 row indicates less than $500,000.\n\nNow I need to find how much paper money was added in circulation in that year (1962).\n\nLooking at the paper money totals:\n- 1961: Total paper money = 29,943 (million)\n- 1962: Total paper money = 31,117 (million)\n\nSo the increase in paper money from 1961 to 1962 = 31,117 - 29,943 = 1,174 million dollars.\n\nWait, but I should look at the question more carefully. The question asks: \"how much paper money was added in circulation\" in the year the treasury notes of 1890 were removed from the ledgers.\n\nLooking at the data more carefully, I see that in 1961 (fiscal year), Treasury notes of 1890 = $1 million, and in 1962, it shows \"*\" which is less than $500,000. This suggests they were removed in fiscal year 1962.\n\nThe increase in total paper money from 1961 to 1962:\n31,117 - 29,943 = 1,174 million dollars\n\n\nI notice a discrepancy between the fiscal year and calendar year data. The monthly breakdown for 1962 shows a more complex picture of currency removal. Specifically, the Treasury notes dropped from $1 million in 1961 to essentially zero in some months of 1962, indicating the removal happened during this period.\n\nI'll investigate whether calendar year 1962 aligns with the fiscal year data. By comparing the paper money circulation between December 1961 and December 1962, I can verify the increase. The calculation shows 32,026 - 31,336 = 690 million, which differs from the fiscal year calculation of 1,174 million.\n\nI need to confirm if fiscal or calendar year data provides the most accurate representation of the currency removal timeline.\n\nThe Treasury notes transitioned from $1 million in 1961 to \"*\" in 1962, indicating a significant change in the fiscal record. I'm focusing on determining the precise paper money addition during this specific period.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_14670506ce4448e8a0233de7","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"grep -r \"1890\" /app/resources/*.txt | head -50"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
