lation. Starting from December 1960 at 30,442, the amounts gradually increase through November 1961, reaching 30,975. The incremental changes show a consistent upward trend, with monthly additions ranging between 29 and 619 million.\n\nHowever, the fiscal year data provides additional context. Comparing June 1960 and June 1961 shows a net increase of 217 million in paper money. I'm focusing on understanding what \"paper money added in circulation\" truly represents in this context.\n\nThe Treasury notes of 1890 were officially removed in October 1961, signaling a significant monetary policy action. I'm now exploring how this removal might connect to the overall changes in paper money circulation during that year.\n\nBy examining the monthly progression from December 1960 to November 1961, I can trace the incremental changes. The total paper money rose from 30,442 to 30,975, representing a net increase of 533 million. This calculation reveals the actual paper money added during the calendar year, accounting for seasonal variations and potential retired currency replacements.\n\nI'll refine my calculation by comparing December 1960 and December 1961 data points to ensure accuracy in tracking the monetary expansion.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_179479550e6a4f358018bbe2","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"grep -A2 \"December\" /app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1962_01_page_82.txt | head -20"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
